{
  "slide-0": {
    "shape-0": {
      "left": 1.1,
      "top": 0.48,
      "width": 9.6,
      "height": 1.74,
      "placeholder_type": "TITLE",
      "default_font_size": 54.0,
      "overlap": {
        "overlapping_shapes": {
          "shape-1": 1.9
        }
      },
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Bayesian Bits: Problem and Research Gap"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-1": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 2.02,
      "width": 9.75,
      "height": 0.45,
      "overlap": {
        "overlapping_shapes": {
          "shape-0": 1.9
        }
      },
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Goal: learn an accuracy-efficiency policy instead of hand-picking pruning and bit-width settings."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-2": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 2.75,
      "width": 3.05,
      "height": 2.85,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Deployment bottleneck",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "Inference cost depends on arithmetic and data movement.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Quantization lowers operand width; pruning removes operations.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "The useful target is a Pareto trade-off, not one compressed model.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-3": {
      "left": 4.45,
      "top": 2.75,
      "width": 3.05,
      "height": 2.85,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Past direction",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "Fixed-bit QAT optimizes one selected precision.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Mixed precision searches layer or tensor bit-widths.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Pruning is usually treated as a separate compression task.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-4": {
      "left": 7.72,
      "top": 2.75,
      "width": 3.05,
      "height": 2.85,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Missing capability",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "The bit-width search space is exponential.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Continuous learned bits may not map to power-of-two hardware.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Pruning and quantization need one trainable formulation.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-5": {
      "left": 2.25,
      "top": 6.28,
      "width": 7.6,
      "height": 0.52,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Bayesian Bits turns bit allocation and pruning into gated optimization",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-6": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 8.18,
      "width": 10.0,
      "height": 0.26,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Source: van Baalen et al., Bayesian Bits: Unifying Quantization and Pruning, NeurIPS 2020 / arXiv:2005.07093"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-7": {
      "left": 15.42,
      "top": 8.28,
      "width": 0.42,
      "height": 0.3,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "1",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "slide-1": {
    "shape-0": {
      "left": 1.1,
      "top": 0.48,
      "width": 9.6,
      "height": 1.74,
      "placeholder_type": "TITLE",
      "default_font_size": 54.0,
      "overlap": {
        "overlapping_shapes": {
          "shape-1": 1.9
        }
      },
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Why Existing Mixed Precision Was Not Enough"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-1": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 2.02,
      "width": 9.5,
      "height": 0.42,
      "overlap": {
        "overlapping_shapes": {
          "shape-0": 1.9
        }
      },
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Earlier methods improved quantization, but each left a deployment gap."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-2": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 2.72,
      "width": 2.32,
      "height": 3.15,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Fixed-bit QAT",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "PACT, LSQ, and TQT learn clipping or step sizes.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Strong at one precision.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "They do not decide where each tensor should spend bits.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-3": {
      "left": 3.72,
      "top": 2.72,
      "width": 2.32,
      "height": 3.15,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Sensitivity search",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "HAWQ-style methods use Hessian signals to rank layers.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "They expose sensitivity.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "A discrete assignment step is still needed.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-4": {
      "left": 6.26,
      "top": 2.72,
      "width": 2.32,
      "height": 3.15,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "NAS / RL search",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "Search methods can include hardware feedback.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "They optimize bit policies.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Cost and policy complexity grow with model size.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-5": {
      "left": 8.8,
      "top": 2.72,
      "width": 2.32,
      "height": 3.15,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Differentiable bits",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "DQ learns continuous bit-widths.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Real hardware needs rounded widths.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Rounding can erase expected savings.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-6": {
      "left": 2.15,
      "top": 6.45,
      "width": 7.9,
      "height": 0.5,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "The paper targets hardware-friendly power-of-two widths by construction",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-7": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 8.18,
      "width": 10.0,
      "height": 0.26,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Source: van Baalen et al., Bayesian Bits: Unifying Quantization and Pruning, NeurIPS 2020 / arXiv:2005.07093"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-8": {
      "left": 15.42,
      "top": 8.28,
      "width": 0.42,
      "height": 0.3,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "2",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "slide-2": {
    "shape-0": {
      "left": 1.1,
      "top": 0.48,
      "width": 9.6,
      "height": 1.74,
      "placeholder_type": "TITLE",
      "default_font_size": 54.0,
      "overlap": {
        "overlapping_shapes": {
          "shape-1": 2.28
        }
      },
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Core Idea: Gated Residual Quantization"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-1": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 1.98,
      "width": 9.6,
      "height": 0.42,
      "overlap": {
        "overlapping_shapes": {
          "shape-0": 2.28
        }
      },
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "A tensor is represented as a low-bit value plus optional quantized residuals."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-2": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 3.05,
      "width": 1.3,
      "height": 0.46,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "2-bit base",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-3": {
      "left": 3.08,
      "top": 3.05,
      "width": 1.45,
      "height": 0.46,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "+ residual 4",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-4": {
      "left": 5.13,
      "top": 3.05,
      "width": 1.45,
      "height": 0.46,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "+ residual 8",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-5": {
      "left": 7.18,
      "top": 3.05,
      "width": 1.65,
      "height": 0.46,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "+ residual 16",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-6": {
      "left": 9.43,
      "top": 3.05,
      "width": 1.45,
      "height": 0.46,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "+ residual 32",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-7": {
      "left": 1.55,
      "top": 3.78,
      "width": 8.9,
      "height": 0.38,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "xq = z2 · (x2 + z4 · (eps4 + z8 · (eps8 + z16 · (eps16 + z32 · eps32))))",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-8": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 4.62,
      "width": 2.32,
      "height": 1.72,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Start low",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "Begin with a 2-bit grid.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "This is the lowest nonzero representation.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-9": {
      "left": 3.72,
      "top": 4.62,
      "width": 2.32,
      "height": 1.72,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Add residuals",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "Each residual halves the quantization error scale.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Effective precision doubles.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-10": {
      "left": 6.26,
      "top": 4.62,
      "width": 2.32,
      "height": 1.72,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Learn gates",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "Stochastic binary gates choose residuals.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Hard-concrete relaxation enables gradients.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-11": {
      "left": 8.8,
      "top": 4.62,
      "width": 2.32,
      "height": 1.72,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Unify pruning",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        },
        {
          "text": "A 0-bit gate sits before the 2-bit value.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        },
        {
          "text": "Turning it off prunes the tensor group.",
          "space_before": 4.0
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-12": {
      "left": 2.05,
      "top": 6.85,
      "width": 8.0,
      "height": 0.5,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Bayesian prior: penalize active gates, optionally in proportion to BOP cost",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-13": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 8.18,
      "width": 10.0,
      "height": 0.26,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Source: van Baalen et al., Bayesian Bits: Unifying Quantization and Pruning, NeurIPS 2020 / arXiv:2005.07093"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-14": {
      "left": 15.42,
      "top": 8.28,
      "width": 0.42,
      "height": 0.3,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "3",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "slide-3": {
    "shape-0": {
      "left": 1.1,
      "top": 0.48,
      "width": 9.6,
      "height": 1.74,
      "placeholder_type": "TITLE",
      "default_font_size": 54.0,
      "overlap": {
        "overlapping_shapes": {
          "shape-1": 2.28
        }
      },
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Observations, Results, and Takeaway"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-1": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 1.98,
      "width": 9.55,
      "height": 0.42,
      "overlap": {
        "overlapping_shapes": {
          "shape-0": 2.28
        }
      },
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "The learned gates behave like a resource allocation policy over precision and channels."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-2": {
      "left": 1.43,
      "top": 2.76,
      "width": 3.25,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Low-bit priors apply useful pressure"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-3": {
      "left": 4.73,
      "top": 2.76,
      "width": 6.2,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "The variational objective regularizes active gates; the prior can be scaled by BOP contribution."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-4": {
      "left": 1.43,
      "top": 3.63,
      "width": 3.25,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Joint pruning + quantization wins"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-5": {
      "left": 4.73,
      "top": 3.63,
      "width": 6.2,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "ImageNet ResNet18 ablations show the combined curve beats pruning-only and quantization-only variants."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-6": {
      "left": 1.43,
      "top": 4.5,
      "width": 3.25,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Known heuristics emerge"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-7": {
      "left": 4.73,
      "top": 4.5,
      "width": 6.2,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Aggressive settings push most tensors to 2-bit while often preserving first and last layers at higher precision."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-8": {
      "left": 1.43,
      "top": 5.37,
      "width": 3.25,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Post-training use is possible"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-9": {
      "left": 4.73,
      "top": 5.37,
      "width": 6.2,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "On pretrained ResNet18, the method can learn gates, or gates plus scales, without updating weights."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-10": {
      "left": 1.43,
      "top": 6.24,
      "width": 3.25,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Cost remains a limitation"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-11": {
      "left": 4.73,
      "top": 6.24,
      "width": 6.2,
      "height": 0.77,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "One ResNet18 run used 30 Bayesian Bits epochs plus 10 fixed-gate fine-tuning epochs, about 70 hours on one Tesla V100."
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-12": {
      "left": 2.0,
      "top": 7.32,
      "width": 8.3,
      "height": 0.5,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Takeaway: pruning becomes 0-bit quantization; precision becomes a learnable budget",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-13": {
      "left": 1.18,
      "top": 8.18,
      "width": 10.0,
      "height": 0.26,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Source: van Baalen et al., Bayesian Bits: Unifying Quantization and Pruning, NeurIPS 2020 / arXiv:2005.07093"
        }
      ]
    },
    "shape-14": {
      "left": 15.42,
      "top": 8.28,
      "width": 0.42,
      "height": 0.3,
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "4",
          "alignment": "CENTER"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}